+Concise Bounded Description
+---------------------------
+
+The Concise Bounded Description (CBD) of a resource is the subgraph that
+describes it: all triples with the resource as subject, plus, recursively,
+the CBD of every blank node reached as an object, and any reification
+statements about those triples. See the `W3C member submission
+<https://www.w3.org/Submission/CBD/>`_ for the full definition.
+
+Use :meth:`~rdflib.graph.Graph.cbd` to extract it. It returns a new
+:class:`~rdflib.graph.Graph` and leaves the source graph unchanged::
+
+    >>> from rdflib import Graph, URIRef, BNode, Literal
+    >>> from rdflib.namespace import FOAF
+    >>> g = Graph()
+    >>> alice = URIRef("http://example.org/alice")
+    >>> addr = BNode()
+    >>> g.add((alice, FOAF.name, Literal("Alice")))
+    >>> g.add((alice, FOAF.based_near, addr))
+    >>> g.add((addr, FOAF.name, Literal("Somewhere")))
+    >>> len(g.cbd(alice))
+    3
+
+Blank nodes reachable from the resource are followed, and cycles between
+blank nodes are handled, so the traversal always terminates.
+
+This is also the building block for SPARQL ``DESCRIBE`` queries.
+
